#: ../../source/docs/tailsitter-tuning-guide.rst:5
#: c5a70eb5e1714700bcfd7da5d6a64615
msgid "Tailsitter VTOL Tuning"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tailsitter-tuning-guide.rst:7
#: eab863a3633540bfa236c10d56cc98b8
msgid "Tuning a tailsitter is different than tuning a normal SLT (Separate Lift Thrust) or Tilt-Rotor Quadplane. Those QuadPlanes tune very similarly to a Multirotor since the attitude in VTOL is controlled by motor speed/thrust in all axes (the exception being YAW in vectored yaw tilt rotor QuadPlanes)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tailsitter-tuning-guide.rst:9
#: 83d5a636428f4a84baae928f14e8caef
msgid "In most tailsitters, VTOL attitude is usually controlled by some combination of fixed wing control surfaces and, in some configurations, motor tilt for pitch and yaw. Roll is usually controlled by motor/speed thrust and can be tuned, and even AutoTuned, like a multicopter and follows the normal :ref:`QuadPlane<quadplane-vtol-tuning>` tuning for that axis. Most of the instructions for tuning setup and phases are the same for Tailsitters, except as described below:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tailsitter-tuning-guide.rst:14
#: 3fe39e9a285d4dc9968a7a2ffb89f530
msgid "Pitch and Yaw Tuning"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tailsitter-tuning-guide.rst:16
#: 31d9a7d9f6b14484a10179941507b776
msgid "For pitch and yaw, control is provided by the fixed wing control surfaces, sometimes in conjunction with motor tilt, depending on type of tailsitter. These axes required a tuning approach more similar to normal fixed wing manual tuning, since the FF (Feed-Forward) component of the PID loop is the primary control path, with P and D PID terms providing disturbance corrections. The tuning process is as follows, starting from the default PID values:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tailsitter-tuning-guide.rst:18
#: 95ebf316722948e4b96dc45cb3dc3da8
msgid "First, follow Steps 1 - 4 of the normal :ref:`QuadPlane Tuning Process Setup<quadplane-vtol-tuning-process>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tailsitter-tuning-guide.rst:19
#: 75d75fba49f740ca898dde356d73b20d
msgid "Normally, the vehicle is stable enough with the default PIDs to do a first test hover in QSTABILIZE or QHOVER. Do a short hover flight with logging enabled such that the :ref:`Harmonic Notch Filters<common-imu-notch-filtering>` can be setup. Its important to remove as much motor noise as possible in order to get a good tune using a throttle based harmonic notch filter."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tailsitter-tuning-guide.rst:20
#: 95b2aa763d554198a7581197254b8da0
msgid "Once the notch filters are set, do another hover flight but briefly do short, but sharp pitch stick movements in both directions. Do not endanger the vehicle, but try to get several short full stick movements to the front and to the back. Do the same for yaw, but you should be able to hold full yaw stick each direction for a second or so, returning to neutral for a second in-between direction reversals."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tailsitter-tuning-guide.rst:21
#: c589f6e4e546461c98498e3183f0ad72
msgid "Download the :ref:`dataflash log<common-downloading-and-analyzing-data-logs-in-mission-planner>` for analysis. In order to determine the :ref:`Q_A_RAT_PIT_FF<Q_A_RAT_PIT_FF>` and :ref:`Q_A_RAT_YAW_FF<Q_A_RAT_YAW_FF>` terms, use a log analyzer like https://plot.ardupilot.org and setup the following plots on the same scale, using the \"Add Expression\" button:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tailsitter-tuning-guide.rst:28
#: b710cd1314a9422a8a6f41b2d8222b6a
msgid "Next adjust the X value above until the magnitudes of both plots are about equal. This will now be value for the :ref:`Q_A_RAT_PIT_FF<Q_A_RAT_PIT_FF>` term. Set the :ref:`Q_A_RAT_PIT_I<Q_A_RAT_PIT_I>` term to equal this."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tailsitter-tuning-guide.rst:29
#: 27edc8fb0ec0485c878351e4d8fa2adf
msgid "Now do the same for the YAW axis using:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tailsitter-tuning-guide.rst:36
#: b8613c563478457787c36b864518fa74
msgid "Below is an image of this where the X value is adjusted until the two curves are the same amplitude, with a value of x = 0.1. This is then set as the :ref:`Q_A_RAT_YAW_FF<Q_A_RAT_YAW_FF>` and :ref:`Q_A_RAT_YAW_I<Q_A_RAT_YAW_I>` values."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tailsitter-tuning-guide.rst:41
#: 41fbc9f85cc2416298346efe1670d5cf
msgid "Now you can hover again, and begin increasing the :ref:`Q_A_RAT_PIT_D<Q_A_RAT_PIT_D>` term, either iteratively, or using :ref:`common-transmitter-tuning`, until it oscillates and then reduce it to 1/2 to 1/3 that value."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tailsitter-tuning-guide.rst:42
#: 1bdd3ae37ba34a7a8059cdfa47b77261
msgid "Then increase the :ref:`Q_A_RAT_PIT_P<Q_A_RAT_PIT_P>` term,, until it oscillates and then reduce it  1/2 to 1/3 that value."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tailsitter-tuning-guide.rst:43
#: 561743ac574243209129693efdf7d040
msgid "Do the same for the YAW axis."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tailsitter-tuning-guide.rst:46
#: 72a878b1b4c54bd692559760e5a6206a
msgid "Roll Tuning"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tailsitter-tuning-guide.rst:48
#: ee99c16069784656babb391af1915ca9
msgid "This should allow you to get a reasonable tune for Pitch and Yaw. Roll is tuned like STEP 10 for Roll in :ref:`QuadPlane<quadplane-vtol-tuning>`. Roll axis can even be AutoTuned using QAUTOTUNE, if restricted to only the roll axis using :ref:`Q_AUTOTUNE_AXES<Q_AUTOTUNE_AXES>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tailsitter-tuning-guide.rst:51
#: 6738c4ee3a1e42c0ae0d06334e0fdd4e
msgid "Using the Quick Tune LUA Applet"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tailsitter-tuning-guide.rst:53
#: ea5bd80e12fe46a990e5f18420f3362b
msgid "For systems using an autopilot with sufficient memory to run :ref:`LUA scripts <common-lua-scripts>`, such as F7 and H7 based controllers, this process has been automated via a LUA Applet for non-Tailsitter class QuadPlanes, which do not use FF PID terms in their tuning. However, it still can be used for Tailsitters on those types of axes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tailsitter-tuning-guide.rst:55
#: 0b9da898cb994558a0058632c6791226
msgid "See the `Quick VTOL Tune LUA script <https://github.com/ArduPilot/ardupilot/blob/master/libraries/AP_Scripting/applets/VTOL-quicktune.md>`__."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tailsitter-tuning-guide.rst:57
#: 64d72630e36f4ecd9c989f2755761df5
msgid "For each FF dominated axis, proceed as above to determine the FF term and set the I term equal to it. Then the applet can be run since it will not change the I term during the process if the FF term is non-zero."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tailsitter-tuning-guide.rst:60
#: a50d2e4e9efa4a2ba184e14f68e5aca9
msgid "VTOL Gain Scaling for Redundant Actuators"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tailsitter-tuning-guide.rst:62
#: 7dc3ff51f40c4f9e9a532abe649aab31
msgid "Some tailsitters have redundant actuators for a given axis. Vectored thrust tailsitters may have both tilting motors for yaw as well as elevons. Copter tailsitters may have fixed wing control surfaces that produce pitch or yaw in addition to the copter style motors. In some cases, it would be desired to have those fixed wing control surfaces provide more attitude control in VTOL than the motors, reducing the thrust/throttle levels needed for that control."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tailsitter-tuning-guide.rst:64
#: 18c758eeadc4499293bc898fc5020e4a
msgid "The following parameters allow the adjustment of how much control is produced the fixed wing control surfaces for each axis. Larger values apply more gain to the control surfaces, this will give the overall system more gain so the ``Q_A_RAT_`` pitch and yaw PID gains may have to be reduced. To reduce the response of the motors, one would for example, half the PID P/D/I/FF rate gains and use a control surface scale factor of two. This would result in the control surfaces responding as before but with the motor outputs halved."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tailsitter-tuning-guide.rst:66
#: a7a5bfd05ecb4ab3856ae2b7573853a9
msgid "Default value is 1, which results in no behavior change from the past. These gains are only active in VTOL modes or under Q assist."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tailsitter-tuning-guide.rst:68
#: 25aa4252647c4f0ba944b216d8403647
msgid ":ref:`Q_TAILSIT_VT_P_P<Q_TAILSIT_VT_P_P>` Scales from Pitch PID output to control surfaces"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tailsitter-tuning-guide.rst:69
#: 637cbb608f9843e2b5cbe37fb49b345f
msgid ":ref:`Q_TAILSIT_VT_R_P<Q_TAILSIT_VT_R_P>` Scales from Roll PID output to control surfaces"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/tailsitter-tuning-guide.rst:70
#: 67acc9d7f66a400998a708893b4683d5
msgid ":ref:`Q_TAILSIT_VT_Y_P<Q_TAILSIT_VT_Y_P>` Scales from Yaw PID output to control surfaces"
msgstr ""
